Design axes as slices of one maximal world (M9-B).
==================================================

Applies ON TOP of theta-mutation.patch and m8-seed-log.patch, in the
disposable clone only:

    cd /home/user/donor-smoke
    git apply .../theta-mutation.patch
    git apply .../m8-seed-log.patch
    git apply .../m9b-axes.patch

This is a diff, not vendored source (D5: the donor has no licence).

WHY
---
M9-B asks what a longer test window or a larger donor pool is worth. That
question only has an answer if the WORLD is held fixed while the DESIGN
moves. The donor's own knobs cannot do that, and the reason is not a bug --
it is that nothing ever asked them to:

  * `draw_baselines()` calls rlnorm(n_geos, ...) and then sort()s. The RNG
    stream is nested, the sorted vector is not: a 6-geo draw is not a prefix
    of a 10-geo draw at the same seed.
  * `select_treated()` picks the geo closest to the MEDIAN baseline, and the
    median moves with pool size. Measured at seed 52001, the treated geo is
        n_geos =  6  ->  City 3   (baseline 2650.4)
        n_geos = 10  ->  City 5   (2760.0)
        n_geos = 21  ->  City 11  (3305.1)
        n_geos = 41  ->  City 21  (3305.1)
  * the noise loop is `for (geo) { for (day) }`, so raising total_days moves
    the stream for every geo after the first. T = 15 is not a prefix of
    T = 42 either.

So a 16-cell sweep over (T, G_c) using the donor's knobs would compare 16
different worlds and call the difference a design effect. Every number would
be internally correct and the axis would mean something false -- the same
class as F13-F19, conceptual-model contract failure.

THE CONTRACT
------------
Every replication generates ONE maximal latent world:

    90 pre days + MAX_POST_DAYS (42) post days
    n_treated (1) + MAX_CONTROLS (40) geos

and each cell is a slice of it:

    treated geo   selected ONCE from the 41-geo world, by the unchanged
                  median-baseline rule. The same city in every cell.
    donor pools   the other 40 geos in a fixed random order; each cell takes
                  a prefix, so D5 subset D9 subset D20 subset D40.
    durations     prefixes of the maximal post-period, so
                  Y15 subset Y21 subset Y28 subset Y42.

The donor order comes from a DEDICATED RNG substream (panel_seed + 900000),
not from the DGP's noise stream and not from the sorted baselines. Sorted
baselines would have been cheaper and wrong twice over: "fewer donors" would
silently also mean "donors closer to the treated geo in size", and the pool
would stop being independent of the treated selection. Both offsets in use
(1e5 noise, 9e5 permutation) are disjoint from panel_seed's range
[52001, 82025] for this study.

WHAT IT ADDS
------------
1. `--post_days N --n_control G`. Both or neither: a partial invocation
   REFUSES, because one axis sliced and the other taken from a donor default
   is not a slice of the same world as its neighbours.

2. MAX_POST_DAYS / MAX_CONTROLS vs requested_post_days / requested_n_control,
   named apart on purpose. The MAX_* pair sizes the world; the requested_*
   pair cuts the slice. If a later "optimisation" generates a world sized to
   the request -- which looks like pure savings, about 1.7 CPU-hours across
   the grid -- every nesting property dies and no downstream statistic
   notices. A stopifnot() in the loop is the tripwire; m9b_world_check.py's
   W6 is the proof.

3. M9B_SEED_SLOT = 1L. panel_seed would otherwise be
   master_seed*1000 + match(sc_id, names(scenarios))*10000 + it, and every
   cell happens to be the sole element of its own scenarios list, so the
   slot would be 1 by accident. Pinned, so "all 16 cells share one world" is
   a stated contract rather than a property of list length.

4. One cell per invocation, id `M9B_T{post}_G{controls}`, on the A1 template
   (no outlier, 90 pre days). Scenario ids are directory names and the join
   key in results.jsonl; a shared id would have cells overwrite each other,
   which is the defect theta-mutation.patch had to fix for effect labels.

5. Seed-log columns: perm_seed, world_n_geos, world_total_days,
   requested_post_days, requested_n_control, treated_geo, donor_order (the
   full 40-geo permutation) and cell_donors. The nesting is therefore
   auditable from the CSV without re-running R. The log is ALSO written
   beside the panels, because results/raw/panel_seeds.csv is a fixed path in
   the CWD and the next cell's run overwrites it -- 15 of 16 would survive
   only by the orchestrator remembering to copy each one out. F11's lesson:
   make losing the evidence structurally impossible, not procedurally
   avoided.

6. In M9-B mode the run exits before the diagnostic plot. That section
   re-derives the treated geo from draw_baselines() at the SCENARIO's geo
   count, which here is the slice size and not the world's -- it would name
   the wrong city and draw a confident, wrong picture. The donor's figure
   layer already lies on any theta != 7.5%; this is the same coupling.
   Refusing to draw beats drawing something false.

LEGACY IS UNTOUCHED
-------------------
With neither flag, `m9b_mode` is FALSE and every changed line is bypassed.
The `else` branch in the main loop is the original Steps 1-4, re-indented by
two spaces and otherwise character-for-character unchanged; the seed-log
record gains its extra columns only inside `if (m9b_mode)`; the one line
that always evaluates differently is

    seed_slot <- if (m9b_mode) M9B_SEED_SLOT else match(sc_id, names(scenarios))

whose else-branch is the original expression.

That is the argument. The evidence is L1 in m9b_world_check.py, which
recovers the pre-M9B file by REVERSE-APPLYING this patch (not by a copy
taken by hand, which would only prove a copy matches itself), runs both
versions with no flags, and compares every output byte.

A1/A3 REPRODUCTION IS NOT AN M9-B ACCEPTANCE CONDITION
-------------------------------------------------------
M9-B cells are deliberately NOT the donor's A1/A3. A1 has 21 geos drawn as
21; M9B_T15_G20 has 21 geos drawn as 41 and sliced. Their numbers differ,
and must: that difference is precisely the confound this patch removes.
Only the LEGACY invocation is required to reproduce A1-A4, and it does,
byte-for-byte. Anyone comparing an M9-B cell against a published A-scenario
number is comparing two different worlds.

VERIFIED, not illustrative
--------------------------
    Rscript parse()     generate_panels.R parses, 67 expressions
    git apply --check   clean against the theta-mutation + m8-seed-log state
                        (donor file sha256 7d690e9b..., the hash the M8
                        freeze records)
    reverse apply       recovers that exact sha256
    live run            see m9b_world_check.py output, reproduced in
                        docs/m9-preregistration.md

WHAT IT DOES NOT CHANGE
-----------------------
No estimator, no tool config, no RNG algorithm, no legacy output. It adds
two flags, one branch, eight seed-log columns and nine metadata fields. What
it does change is what a cell-to-cell difference is ALLOWED TO MEAN, and
that change is the entire point.

